  "raw": {
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire---College-of-Professional-Studies/Senior-Online-Professional-Faculty_JR9783": {
      "title": "Senior Online Professional Faculty",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app UNH College of Professional Studies Online is currently seeking a full-time faculty member for our Liberal Arts, Communication, and General Education Academic Center. This faculty will lead our Credit for Prior learning program in addition to teaching in our general education program. This role is responsible for developing and scaling evidence-informed approaches for evaluating college-level learning acquired outside traditional academic settings, including portfolio assessment, institutional and industry training, certifications, military learning, civic and workforce education, and professional development. Working within UNH as well as with statewide partners, the faculty in this role will build sustainable pathways connecting non-credit prior and ongoing learning to degree and certificate programs. This position is non-tenure track, and will include a combined administrative and teaching function. Actual course load will vary with other responsibilities. Job Duties/Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership and Systemwide Implementation    Develop, lead, and scale transparent, accessible, portable, and academically rigorous assessment models across the University  and with statewide partners .   Establish frameworks and procedures for portfolio assessment, examinations, institutional and industry evaluations, certifications, military learning, apprenticeships, and civic and workforce training.   Ensure academic credit is awarded for demonstrated college-level learning and aligned with academic policy, accreditation, faculty governance, and institutional standards.   Program and Pathway Alignment   Lead efforts to map prior and ongoing learning to credit-bearing degree, certificate, transfer, civic, and workforce pathways.   Collaborate with faculty and academic leaders to review, approve, document, and maintain credit equivalencies.   Faculty, Staff, and Institutional Engagement   Partner with faculty, academic departments, advisors, registrars, admissions, student success teams, civic and workforce units, and college leadership.   Lead online learning communities, pilot projects, faculty evaluator networks, and teams that provide training, consultation, and technical assistance.   Policy, Compliance, and Risk Management   Ensure compliance with academic policy, accreditation standards, accessibility requirements, and applicable state and federal regulations.   Ensure student communications clearly explain credit application, costs, timelines, appeals, residency, transfer, financial aid, and licensing considerations.   Technology, Systems, and Partnerships   Lead the development of integrated technology-supported processes from student inquiry through evaluation, credit award, transcript posting, and reporting.   Promote effective use of digital portfolios, credential verification tools, equivalency databases, knowledge bases, workflow systems, and analytics platforms.   Student Access and Support   Make prior and ongoing learning opportunities visible, accessible, and understandable to prospective and enrolled students.   Develop advising and referral processes that help students identify eligible learning assessment options, requirements, costs, timelines, and academic implications.   Data, Performance, and Continuous Improvement   Establish reporting and key performance indicators for participation, credit awards, retention, completion, equity, student satisfaction, and program impact.   Use data, student feedback, market research, and resource capacity to guide responsible program expansion, resource allocation, and process improvement.   Requirements: Minimum Education & Experience: Master’s degree and five or more years of experience in teaching, prior learning assessment, professional development or related fields is required. Documented project management experience Knowledge Skills & Abilities: This position requires a commitment to teaching excellence and a high-level of student service. A strong record of online teaching experience and demonstrated expertise with credit for prior learning is required. Proficiency with current technology standards such as CRM, e-portfolios, LMS, and other higher education systems is needed. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral or terminal degree in a business related area (e.g. Human Resources, Accounting, Finance, Management, Information Technology) PMP certification Applications received by 5 pm on Monday, September 28, 2026 will receive priority review. Compensation Pay Range: $74,000-83,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Manchester Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire---College-of-Professional-Studies/Sr-Lecturer_JR9667": {
      "title": "Sr Lecturer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 27 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The American Sign Language/English Interpreting Program of the University of New Hampshire at Manchester seeks applicants for a full-time, lecturer position to offer instruction as well as to serve as the ASL/English Interpreting program director. Our mission is to develop learners with the knowledge and skills to flourish in the academic environment and in employment. To this end, the position encompasses the need for excellence in instruction in skill-specific courses pertaining to interpreting as well as ethics and decision-making and other relevant topics. It requires knowledge and experience in pedagogy, scholarly and practical developments in the field, and with the past and current state of the U.S. Deaf community. Responsibilities: Coordinate all aspects of the ASL/English Interpreting program including supervision of and collaboration with the assistant director and the pool of adjunct faculty.  Revise/update curriculum as needed, Serve as an informal advisor to students in the program, Create and/or participate in recruitment efforts, Coordinate program events, Maintain and support connections with the Deaf community and with interpreting colleagues both locally and nationally Engage in activity across the college in support of the overall institution.  The person will also teach six courses in ASL/English translation or interpretation. The teaching load is 3 courses per semester, and courses range from introductory to advanced courses in skill-development and relevant content areas. Minimum Qualifications Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: A masters degree in Interpretation, Linguistics, or a related field, is required as are excellence in teaching, and experience in administration and interpreter education. Applicants also must be certified interpreters who are active in the interpreting profession. Required Licenses & Certifications: Active national certification as granted by the Registry of Interpreters for the Deaf or National Association of the Deaf is required. Membership and participation in Conference for Interpreter Trainers would also be beneficial. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Learning Management Systems such as Canvas or Blackboard and other technology-based teaching tools such as GoReact  and TWA is also useful. Expertise in various interpreting models, history of the profession, current dynamics in the field and in the Deaf, Hard of Hearing, DeafBlind and Deaf+ communities, and cultural and socio-political perspectives of the communities and the profession. Knowledge of field specific research both historic and current. Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to network and recruit language models for classroom practice and discussions. Preferred Qualifications: PhD Applicant Instructions Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - If more than 5 documents are required, upload additional documents within the \"Application Questions\" section.)  Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Teaching Philosophy The committee will begin reviewing applications starting October 9, 2026, and continue reviewing until the position is filled. Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration.  Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range $80,000.00 - $87,000.00/Annual The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Manchester Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire---College-of-Professional-Studies/Adjunct-Faculty---Electrical-Engineering_JR7535": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Electrical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-04-23\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The UNH Manchester College of Professional Studies invites applications for adjunct faculty positions to teach undergraduate Electrical Engineering Technology courses and labs beginning the fall semester. Courses include AC/DC circuit analysis, microprocessor technology, analog circuits, digital circuits and electronic devices. These are 16-week, on-campus offerings. Duties/Responsibilities 3-hour weekly lectures, class meets in person for two 1½ hour sessions per week; and 2-hour weekly laboratory sessions. (50%) Maintain course materials through the Learning Management System (LMS). (30%) Proctor exams, grade homework and laboratory reports in a timely manner. (20%) Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Minimum requirements include a master’s degree in electrical engineering or closely related field from a regionally accredited institution and previous college teaching experience preferred. All faculty positions have as their primary objective to create a dynamic teaching/learning environment for our students.  Demonstrated knowledge of electrical engineering including current developments and technologies in the field of electrical engineering, or a closely related field is a requirement. Ability to teach and mentor students in a laboratory setting is a must. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s Degree in Electrical Engineering or closely related field. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents) Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Compensation Pay Range: $ 4000 - $5500 per 4 credit course based on qualifications The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Manchester Salary Grade: Period Activity"
    },
